title: Overview - Parse Pipeline (v1.0)

What happens when a tiddler with custom markup is parsed: the registry boots, vocabs activate, core rules get amended, and the master regex starts matching — with the active-set filter switched on only after pragmas have had their chance.

[img[cm-v1-parse-flow.svg]]

!! The pipeline, step by step

; 1. `cmblock.init()` — bootstrap the registry
: `parser.cmRegistry = new CmRegistry(wiki)` is created once per parser. Four kick-off calls:
:* `loadAllMarkers()` — every `$:/tags/CustomMarkup/Marker` tiddler (shadows + tiddlers) gets parsed into the `markers` map.
:* `loadGlobalPragmas()` — bridges `$:/tags/Pragma` tiddlers through `$:/config/custom-markup/pragma/PageTemplate`'s `\importcustom`. Parsed pragmas are cached per wiki (~3× faster).
:* `activateFromTypeField(type)` — reads `;vocab=A,B` from the tiddler's `type`, calls `activate()` for each. With no `;vocab=`, activates `Default` alone.
:* `applyAmendRules(parser)` — ''schedules'' core-rule removal. Can't call `parser.amendRules` from `init()` directly because the block / inline / pragma rule arrays aren't all assigned yet (RSOD). Instead a one-shot wrap of `parser.parsePragmas` runs the amendment safely.

; 2. Build the master regex
: `cmblock.matchRegExp = registry.getBlockRegex()` and `cminline.matchRegExp = registry.getInlineRegex()` cover ''every'' known marker, not just active ones. This is what survives TW's `instantiateRules` pruning (rules whose `findNextMatch` returns `undefined` get dropped), so pragma-activated vocabs still fire after pragmas run.

; 3. `parsePragmas` (wrapped)
: Inside the one-shot wrap:
:* `parser.amendRules("except", disabledCoreRules)` removes the union of all activated vocabs' `disable-core-rules` (Fountain: `heading list`; Markdown: `strikethrough codeinline list underscore table`; ...).
:* The original `parsePragmas` runs. User pragmas in source can amend further:
:** `\custom <kind>=<sym> ...` adds / overrides a marker locally (auto-activates).
:** `\importcustom [filter]` activates additional vocabs (or imports v0.x `\custom` pragmas).
:** `\debugmarker <open>=<mode>` overrides a marker's `debug` field for this tiddler.
:* On completion, `parser._cmPragmasDone = true` is set and the cached `matchIndex` for `cmblock` / `cminline` / `markdown-table` / `markdown-newline` is refreshed (TW caches `matchIndex` from before pragmas — stale otherwise).

; 4. `parseBlocks` / `parseInlineRun` — match, filter, render
: `findNextMatch(pos)` scans the source with the master regex. ''After'' `_cmPragmasDone`, a match whose marker isn't in the `active` set is skipped (regex position advances by 1 and the scan continues). Without this filter, an inactive marker whose `open` collides with a TW core rule (e.g. Fountain's `#` SECTION vs core `list`) would silently consume the position via the text-fallback in `parse()`, suppressing the core rule for every tiddler in the wiki. Active matches are claimed; `parse()` consumes the body per `kind` (newline / blank-line / close literal / `end-string`), emits the configured element with `wltc` + `wltc-l<N>` + marker `classes`, and scrubs attributes (sandbox iframe, deny `object`/`embed`/`frame`, strip `javascript:` URLs).

; 5. Parse tree → widget tree → DOM
: TW takes over. Markers with `debug` (or a `\debugmarker` override) emit a code block alongside (or instead of) the normal render.

!! Why the multi-stage dance?

TW's `WikiParser` prunes block / inline rules whose `findNextMatch` returns `undefined` at instantiation. `amendRules()` can only run after all rule arrays exist. So:

* The registry loads ''every'' marker up-front, so `cmblock` and `cminline` always have a usable regex and survive pruning.
* Cross-rule cooperation (amending TW core rules) is deferred via a one-shot `parsePragmas` wrap.
* The active-set filter in `findNextMatch` is gated on `_cmPragmasDone` so pragma-activated vocabs still take effect for body parsing.

This is what lets `\importcustom`, in-source `\custom` overrides, type-field `;vocab=...`, and global PageTemplate pragmas all compose without stomping on TW's core wikitext rules outside the tiddlers that opt in.

